<p>
I&rsquo;m still under the weather: mostly I&rsquo;ve been sleeping non-stop except for meals and bathroom breaks. That means that I&rsquo;m still not up to producing a post with the usual Irreal wit and charm<sup><a id="fnr.1" class="footref" href="#fn.1" role="doc-backlink">1</a></sup>. I did have this <a href="https://curtismchale.ca/2026/10/07/emacs-carnival-will-we-change-how-we-interact-with-emacs">post from Curtis McHale about the future of Emacs interaction</a> in my TODO queue so I decided to do a quick write up about it.
</p>
<p>
McHale&rsquo;s contention is that we use keyboards because they&rsquo;re the best way of interfacing with computers. He points to everybody&rsquo;s favorite keyboard replacement, voice input, but notes that even on Star Trek—that arguably popularized the idea—Captain Picard still tells his crew members where he wants to go and <i>they</i> type in the commands at their control stations.
</p>
<p>
As I said in <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/">my last post on the matter</a>. my written thoughts are <i>always</i> much better organized than my verbal ones so moving to spoken input would be a net loss for me. Still, it will be interesting to see how things play out.
</p>
